From: Steam-Link-PS5
Subject: frame stats: do not report a network duration across unsynchronised clocks

The aggregator folds "frame send" (host clock) minus "frame receive" (local
monotonic clock) into accumulated stat 6. Without a clock offset estimate that
value is the offset between the two clocks (tens of millions of milliseconds
here), the host logs "Slow framerate ... (network)" and drops nearly every
frame. Skip the stat until ihslib can estimate the offset.

diff -ru a/src/session/frame_stats.c b/src/session/frame_stats.c
--- a/src/session/frame_stats.c	2026-10-08 17:01:34.255289291 -0700
+++ b/src/session/frame_stats.c	2026-10-08 19:46:09.897657041 -0700
@@ -190,11 +190,11 @@
         agg->lastFrameTimestamp = slot->events[EV_COMPLETE];
     }
 
-    /* Network duration (stat 6): event 12 → 13. */
-    if (has_event(slot, EV_FRAME_SEND) && has_event(slot, EV_FRAME_RECV)) {
-        accum_add(accum, 6,
-                  ticks_to_ms(slot->events[EV_FRAME_SEND], slot->events[EV_FRAME_RECV]));
-    }
+    /* Network duration (stat 6): event 12 → 13. Event 12 is stamped with the
+     * host's clock and event 13 with ours, and nothing synchronises the two,
+     * so the difference is the clock offset (hours), not a network delay.
+     * Steam reads it as "network is the bottleneck" and drops nearly every
+     * frame. Leave the stat out until a clock offset estimate exists. */
     /* Decode duration (stat 7): event 14 → 15. */
     if (has_event(slot, EV_DECODE_BEGIN) && has_event(slot, EV_DECODE_END)) {
         accum_add(accum, 7,
